<article class="post-body">

<!-- §01 — THE PROBLEM -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Long-Trajectory Problem</div>
<h2>Why Test-Time Scaling Breaks for Agents</h2>
<p>
  Test-time scaling is remarkably simple for short-output problems: run the model multiple times, collect candidate answers, and pick the majority vote or the highest-scoring one. This works beautifully for math (compare "42", "41", "42" → pick 42) and even for single-function code generation (compare 50-line functions → run tests → pick the passing one).
</p>
<p>
  Agentic coding destroys this paradigm. To fix a real bug in a codebase, an agent reads files, edits code, runs tests, inspects error messages, and repeats — often for 40+ sequential steps. The output isn't an answer you can compare: it's a long, noisy <em>trajectory</em> of interleaved actions and observations.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">compare-attempts.canvas — "which attempt is best?"</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to try selecting</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="problem-canvas" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;margin-top:.875rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <button id="prob-math-btn" class="ctx-btn active" data-mode="math">Math reasoning</button>
      <button id="prob-code-btn" class="ctx-btn" data-mode="code">Code generation</button>
      <button id="prob-agent-btn" class="ctx-btn" data-mode="agent">Agentic coding</button>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin:.625rem 0 0;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">
      Hover a column to highlight it — for agentic coding, you can't simply rank the candidates
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The core tension: in math, each candidate is a compact string you can reason about. In agentic coding, each candidate is thousands of tokens — bash commands, file reads, error logs, partial patches. You can't load all 16 trajectories into a single context window and ask "which one is best?" The signal is buried under noise.
</p>
<p>
  The paper's central thesis is that this is a <strong>representation problem</strong>. The raw trajectory is the wrong object to compare. You need to transform it first.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 — ROLLOUT SUMMARIES -->
<section id="s-summary" data-code="summary">
<div class="section-no">02 — Rollout Summaries as Reusable Currency</div>
<h2>Compress Before You Compare</h2>
<p>
  The key idea is deceptively simple: after each rollout, ask the same model to produce a <strong>compact structured summary</strong> of what just happened. This summary becomes the universal interface — the substrate for comparison (RTV) and the input for refinement (PDR).
</p>
<p>
  A good summary captures the four things you actually need to know: what hypothesis the agent was testing, what it found, why it failed, and what remains to try. Everything else — repeated terminal output, dead-end local explorations, redundant file reads — is discarded.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">summarize.py — trajectory → structured summary</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click steps to see the fields</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="summary-steps">
      <div class="step-box active" data-step="0">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Hypothesis</div>
          <div class="step-desc">What the agent thinks the bug is. Example: <em>"Off-by-one error in <code>parser.py</code> line 142 — the slice end index excludes the last character"</em></div>
          <div class="step-code">hypothesis: "slice end index off-by-one in parse_token()"</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-step="1">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Progress</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Concrete steps taken and what they revealed. <em>"Located root cause, confirmed with targeted print debugging. Partial fix applied to <code>parse_token()</code>."</em></div>
          <div class="step-code">progress: "root cause confirmed; partial fix at parser.py:142"</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-step="2">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Failure Mode</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Why the attempt didn't pass. <em>"<code>test_parse_edge_cases</code> still fails — empty string input raises IndexError, not handled by the fix."</em></div>
          <div class="step-code">failure: "empty-string edge case not handled → IndexError"</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-step="3">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Remaining</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Actionable next steps for a subsequent agent. <em>"Add guard for empty input. Also: verify <code>test_parse_unicode</code> passes after fix — wasn't run."</em></div>
          <div class="step-code">remaining: "add empty-string guard; run unicode tests"</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="sum-prev" disabled>← prev</button>
      <button id="sum-next">next →</button>
      <span id="sum-prog" style="font-size:.68rem;color:var(--muted);font-family:var(--ff-mono)">Field 1 of 4</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The paper tests two substrates for comparison: <strong>raw trajectories</strong> (all the noisy action/observation pairs) vs. <strong>structured summaries</strong>. Summaries win decisively — especially in the final tournament round where the remaining candidates are close to each other and nuanced differences matter most. At that stage, full trajectories are too noisy to discriminate; the summary has already extracted the signal.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Summarization (Eq. 4)</div>
  S<sub>i</sub> = LLM[ P<sub>sum</sub>( R<sub>i</sub> ) ]<br>
  <span class="math-comment">— given rollout R_i, produce compact summary S_i</span><br>
  <span class="math-comment">— S_i is then used as substrate for both RTV and PDR</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- §03 — RTV -->
<section id="s-rtv" data-code="rtv">
<div class="section-no">03 — Recursive Tournament Voting (RTV)</div>
<h2>Eliminate the Weak, Keep the Strong</h2>
<p>
  Given N parallel rollouts and their summaries, how do you pick the best one without access to ground-truth test results? RTV answers this with a tournament: instead of loading all N summaries at once (which overwhelms the model's context and leads to poor decisions), it splits them into small groups of G=2, picks a winner from each group, and repeats until one rollout remains.
</p>
<p>
  Each pairwise comparison uses V=8 votes to reduce noise — the model's judgment about "which attempt is closer to solving this bug" is aggregated over 8 independent runs, and the majority wins. The key finding: <strong>pairwise comparisons (G=2) beat every other group size</strong>, including flat N-way comparisons. Local decisions, stacked recursively, are more reliable than trying to rank everything at once.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">rtv.canvas — tournament with N=8 rollouts</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Next Round to advance</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="rtv-canvas" style="width:100%;height:300px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div class="tournament-controls">
      <button id="rtv-reset">↺ Reset</button>
      <button id="rtv-next">Next Round →</button>
      <span class="tournament-round-badge" id="rtv-round-label">Round 0 — 8 rollouts</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The tournament reduces a population of 16 rollouts to 1 in just 4 rounds — each round making 8 pairwise comparisons with 8 votes each, so 64 model calls per round. The selectivity compounds: a rollout that wins Round 1 was better than its pair; winning Round 2 means it was better than 4 alternatives; winning all 4 means it was better than 15. This compounding is exactly what makes recursive selection more powerful than a single large-group comparison.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">RTV selection (Eq. 5)</div>
  g<sub>j</sub><sup>(r)</sup> = argmax<sub>g</sub> Σ<sub>v</sub> 𝟙[ LLM[ P<sub>comp</sub>(Pin; S<sub>j,1</sub>, S<sub>j,2</sub>) ] = g ]<br>
  <span class="math-comment">— G=2 group, V=8 votes; majority selects the winner</span><br>
  <span class="math-comment">— repeat for ⌈log₂N⌉ rounds until 1 rollout remains</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- §04 — PDR -->
<section id="s-pdr" data-code="pdr">
<div class="section-no">04 — Parallel-Distill-Refine (PDR)</div>
<h2>The Next Generation Learns from the Last</h2>
<p>
  RTV selects the best rollout from a fixed population. But what if the best rollout in that population is still wrong? PDR adds a second dimension: <strong>sequential refinement</strong>. Rather than discarding prior attempts, it feeds their summaries to the next generation of rollouts as conditioning context.
</p>
<p>
  The mechanism: after iteration 0, RTV selects the top K=4 summaries. Each iteration-1 rollout starts with those 4 summaries already in its context — it knows what hypotheses were tried, what partial progress was made, and what failure modes to avoid. The result is that iteration-1 rollouts are "pre-briefed" and take roughly <strong>half as many steps</strong> to reach a solution (Claude Opus: 41 steps → 14 steps on SWE-Bench Verified).
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">pdr.canvas — context quality predicts iteration-1 success</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a context quality to see the pass rate</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="pdr-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div class="ctx-quality-btns">
      <button class="ctx-btn active" data-q="0">0/4 pass</button>
      <button class="ctx-btn" data-q="1">1/4 pass</button>
      <button class="ctx-btn" data-q="2">2/4 pass</button>
      <button class="ctx-btn" data-q="3">3/4 pass</button>
      <button class="ctx-btn" data-q="4">4/4 pass</button>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin:.625rem 0 0;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">
      Claude 4.5 Opus — SWE-Bench Verified — iteration-1 pass@1 by context quality
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The data from Table 5 is striking: when all 4 context rollouts pass (4/4), the next generation succeeds 99.2% of the time. When none pass (0/4), success drops to 0.1%. The quality of the refinement context is the single strongest predictor of next-generation performance — which is exactly why RTV (which selects high-quality context) plus PDR is stronger than random-K PDR.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">PDR refinement context (Eq. 8)</div>
  C<sub>i</sub><sup>(t+1)</sup> = &#123; S<sub>j</sub><sup>(t)</sup> | j ∈ J<sub>i</sub><sup>(t+1)</sup> &#125;  &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; |J| = K = 4<br>
  <span class="math-comment">— J_i is the set of K=4 selected indices (via RTV or random)</span><br>
  A<sub>i,0</sub><sup>(t+1)</sup> = LLM[ P<sub>action</sub>(Pin; C<sub>i</sub><sup>(t+1)</sup>) ]<br>
  <span class="math-comment">— first action conditioned on prior summaries + original problem</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- §05 — FULL PIPELINE -->
<section id="s-pipeline" data-code="pipeline">
<div class="section-no">05 — The Full Pipeline</div>
<h2>Exploitation and Exploration in Balance</h2>
<p>
  RTV and PDR are orthogonal. RTV improves <em>selection within</em> a population. PDR improves <em>reuse across</em> iterations. Combining them gives a 4-stage pipeline that alternates between the two:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Iteration 0</strong> — run N=16 independent rollouts, summarize each one</li>
  <li><strong>Select-K</strong> — RTV tournament to pick K=4 best summaries (exploitation)</li>
  <li><strong>Iteration 1</strong> — run N=16 fresh rollouts, each conditioned on those K summaries (exploration)</li>
  <li><strong>Final RTV</strong> — tournament on iteration-1 rollouts to select the top-1 output</li>
</ul>

